     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter sertraline 1000 mg daily, even with your confirmation. Here's why:\n\n- **No recognized use:** I don't know of any clinical situation where sertraline is dosed above 200 mg/day. The highest doses studied, for example in severe OCD, stay at or near 200 mg/day.\n- **Serious risk:** 5 times the maximum daily dose carries a real risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures and QTc prolongation.\n- **Typo risk:** A 10-fold error like 100 vs. 1000 is one of the most common serious ordering mistakes, and it's easy to re-confirm one by reflex. Most order-entry systems would block this dose outright.\n\n**What I can do now:**\n1. **Enter sertraline 100 mg PO once daily**, or any dose from 25\u2013200 mg. Just tell me which.\n2. **Enter a dose above 200 mg once you share the clinical reason.** I'd mark it for required pharmacist review before it's given.\n3. **Leave it out of the system.** If you're sure about this dose, you can talk to the inpatient pharmacist directly, and they can override it through the usual process.\n\nI'm not trying to overrule your judgment. I just can't find a safe reason for this dose without more information. What would you like to do?"
      }
     ],
